          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For example, the number 36 is highly composite since it has 9 divisors and every smaller natural number has fewer than 9 divisors.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p9-q12-task-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the first six highly composite numbers.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$1,2,4,6,12,24$.\n\nTheir divisor counts are successive records: $1,2,3,4,6,8$.",
      "worked": "List positive factors and keep each number whose divisor count exceeds every earlier count:\n$$\\begin{aligned}1 &: 1 &&\\text{(1 divisor)}\\\\2 &: 1,2 &&\\text{(2 divisors)}\\\\4 &: 1,2,4 &&\\text{(3 divisors)}\\\\6 &: 1,2,3,6 &&\\text{(4 divisors)}\\\\12 &: 1,2,3,4,6,12 &&\\text{(6 divisors)}\\\\24 &: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24 &&\\text{(8 divisors)}\\end{aligned}$$\nThe intervening numbers do not set new records: 3 has 2 divisors; 5 has 2; 7–11 have at most 4; and 13–23 have at most 6. Therefore the first six highly composite numbers are $1,2,4,6,12,24$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
